# Access control invariants

Access control is the primary graded quality attribute (spec Section 7). These invariants come
directly from `docs/requirements/project-requirements.md` Sections 2 and 3, which are
[NORMATIVE] — do not redesign them. Any change touching profile sections, role resolution,
permission checks, or resourcing/profile-sharing flows must be checked against
`docs/access-control/section-matrix.md` and the rules below before it is considered done.

Ported from `.claude/rules/access-control-invariants.md` — keep both in sync by hand if either
changes.

## Two independent dimensions — never collapse them

- **Access roles** (Employee, Manager, People Partner) answer "what data can this person see
  about that person." They are **derived from relationships**, never stored, never assigned.
- **Functional roles** (Unit Manager, DM, PM, PP, HR Admin, and any custom role created later)
  answer "what features does this person get." They are **assigned, stored data**, extensible at
  runtime by HR Admin with no deploy.
- **As of the v1.5 spec changelog, HR Admin is not a data-access role at all** — configuration-only
  (custom fields, dictionaries, departments, functional roles/permissions), no standing read/write
  over any profile section. Full read/write everywhere is a separate, journaled **Full profile
  access** grant (spec §2.4): only an existing holder can grant it, no self-assignment, first
  holder seeded at deployment, last holder can never be removed.
- **A write requires both dimensions to permit it (v1.5).** The section matrix says what an access
  role may read/write; a functional-role permission grant says who may perform a given action —
  both must pass wherever an action is also permission-gated.
- A functional role never widens data access on its own. If a feature needs data, it operates
  within the holder's existing access role.
- Never hardcode a role name (`"DM"`, `"UnitManager"`, `"HRAdmin"`, …) as a permission check
  anywhere in application code. Access roles are resolved from relationships at request time;
  functional-role permissions are resolved from stored, runtime-editable grants.

## Access roles are computed per request, per relationship

- Manager access is the transitive closure of **three** relations as of v1.5: **reports-to**,
  **department management**, and **is assigned to a project managed by**. Reports-to/department
  relations form the **Reporting line**; project-assignment relations form the **Project line** —
  as of v1.5 these are no longer equivalent for data access.
- The same person can be Manager with respect to profile A, People Partner with respect to
  profile B, and a plain colleague with respect to profile C, all within the same session. Never
  cache or compute "the current user's role" as a single global value — resolve it per subject
  profile, per section, per request.
- **The "HR line" (v1.5)** is the people partner's own manager chain *inside HR*, recursive,
  without limit — not the subject employee's chain. Resolving PP access via the employee's own
  reporting line is a distinct bug from a Manager-access miscalculation.
- When a project assignment ends, derived managerial access ends with it immediately — not sticky.
  Revocation timing is split (v1.5): platform-owned relationship edits take effect on the *next
  request*; project-derived access changes within **15 minutes**, degrading to a forced withdrawal
  within **4 hours** if timetracker sync itself is failing. Any cache must invalidate within these
  bounds — a stale permission cache is a data leak.
- **Two documented exceptions to "a Manager sees everything" as of v1.5** (was one): (1) a PM is a
  flag-gated reader on S7 management notes, not a full read-write holder like UM/DM/PP; (2) the
  **Project line is narrowed** — PM/DM via project assignment lose S2/S3 entirely and get S5 as
  CV+certificates only, everything else (incl. S6) identical to the Reporting line, which is not
  narrowed.

## Organisational relationship changes are a distinct, journaled operation (v1.5)

- **Manager, people partner, and department are not writable through S1.** These, plus a
  department's manager, are access switches, not profile data — changing one is a dedicated
  operation with its own permission and screen, never a side effect of a general S1 edit. No
  self-assignment.
- **A narrow journal exists** (spec §3.4) — not a general audit log. It records exactly six event
  types: manager/PP/department changes, department-manager changes, Full profile access grants,
  and shared-link accesses. Any code path performing one of these must write a journal entry as
  part of the same operation.

## Section-level access is enforced server-side, always

- No profile-level permission — every section (S1–S16) is gated independently, per audience, per
  `docs/access-control/section-matrix.md`.
- A section the viewer has no access to must not reach them through **any** surface: API response,
  export, search result, notification, or error message. Never implement a `—` cell by hiding a
  field client-side.

## Colleague view is a whitelist, not a blacklist

A colleague sees exactly: S1 (Identity card), S10 (Leaves — **dates only, no type as of v1.5**),
S11 (project name only). Implement as "return only these fields," not "hide the rest." **One
exception (v1.5):** a campaign author sees name and completion status for their own campaign's
recipients only (S14), nothing else, until the campaign closes.

## S7 Management notes

- Every note carries two independent flags, both **off by default**: `visible for employee` and
  `visible for PM`.
- UM, DM, and PP always have full RW regardless of flags. The employee sees only records flagged
  visible for them; a PM sees only records flagged visible for PM, read-only.
- Where a viewer reaches the same subject through multiple relationship paths, the most-permissive
  resolved access wins — never compute S7 access from an arbitrarily-chosen single path.

## Custom fields and filters must not leak

Every custom field has its own visibility (`management`/`employee`/`colleague`). Filters and list
columns on All Employees — including the on-screen column value, not just filter eligibility —
must respect field visibility.

## Definition of done, for this specific concern

A change touching profile sections, role resolution, or permissions is not done until it matches
`docs/access-control/section-matrix.md` cell-for-cell, has negative tests for every `—` cell it
touches (plus the S7 unflagged case against both employee and PM), and reads functional-role
permissions from stored grants rather than a hardcoded role name.
